    "https://recruiting.ultipro.ca/GOV5000GOVE/JobBoard/b0aac1dd-538d-4eb2-91aa-e8ce062974c9/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=8150ed93-bdb6-44fc-aa7e-946587e13f4b": {
      "title": "Part-time Instructor",
      "description": "Part-time Instructor - ADED 565 - Reading and Critiquing Research in Adult Education (Health Cohort) | Career Opportunities Skip to main content Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Part-time Instructor - ADED 565 - Reading and Critiquing Research in Adult Education (Health Cohort) Job Category : Faculty and Academic Staff Requisition Number : PARTT001767 Apply now Posting Details Posted : October 8, 2026 Part-Time Remote Locations Showing 1 location Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description The Department of Adult Education in the Faculty of Education invites applications for a Part-time Academic Instructor for the following course: Course Number and Title: ADED 565 - Reading and Critiquing Research in Adult Education Course Description: Learners will engage with academic research through a guided, purposeful approach so as to enhance both learner confidence and ability to understand peer reviewed scholarship in the field of adult education. Learners will develop techniques to analyze and critique published research that applies directly to learners’ professional life. The course will guide the learner through the process of research with a “consumer’s” focus, preparing the learner to discern and utilize research in their own practice. The completion of a critical analysis of research studies is a requirement of this course Start and End Dates: January 4 - March 26, 2027 (subject to final budgetary approval) Class Schedule: Thursdays from 5:00-8:00pm Delivery Mode: Online Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or a MAdEd with significant experience in adult education. Application Instructions To apply for this opportunity, click the “Quick Apply” button and upload your cover letter and resume as a single combined PDF document (maximum 6MB). Only one file can be uploaded. Review of applications will begin on October 23, 2026 and continue until the position has been filled. St. Francis Xavier University is located in Mi’kma’ki, the ancestral and unceded territory of the Mi’kmaq People. Our institution is committed to upholding the values of equity, diversity, inclusion and accessibility. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been historically disadvantaged and marginalized, including Indigenous persons (especially Mi’kmaq), racialized persons (especially African Nova Scotians), persons with disabilities, those who identify as women and/or 2SLGBTQIA+ and any others who would contribute to the diversity of our community. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. We are also committed to the elimination of barriers to participation for persons with disabilities. Should you require an accommodation during the recruitment process, please contact People and Culture at hr@stfx. Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications St. Francis Xavier University You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.ca/GOV5000GOVE/JobBoard/b0aac1dd-538d-4eb2-91aa-e8ce062974c9/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=cda4284e-8eca-476e-be76-40e58476fb02": {
      "title": "Part-time Instructor",
      "description": "Part-time Instructor - ADED 585 - Planning Programs for Adults (Health Cohort) | Career Opportunities Skip to main content Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Part-time Instructor - ADED 585 - Planning Programs for Adults (Health Cohort) Job Category : Faculty and Academic Staff Requisition Number : PARTT001766 Quick apply Posting Details Posted : October 8, 2026 Part-Time Remote Locations Showing 1 location Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description The Department of Adult Education in the Faculty of Education invites applications for a Part-time Academic Instructor for the following course: Course Number and Title: ADED 585 - Planning Programs for Adults Course Description: This course focuses on a core area of adult education, program planning theory and practice. The same social, cultural, political and economic factors that influence other human social endeavours are found in program planning; complex planning processes both influence and are influenced by various contexts, behaviours, locations and purposes. Students will engage with program planning’s main theorists and practitioners and will be encouraged to apply theoretical understandings to their practice and in working in diverse contexts and learning communities Start and End Dates: January 4 - March 26, 2027  (subject to final budgetary approval) Class Schedule: Thursdays from 5:00-8:00pm Delivery Mode: Online Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or a MAdEd with significant experience in adult education. Application Instructions To apply for this opportunity, click the “Quick Apply” button and upload your cover letter and resume as a single combined PDF document (maximum 6MB). Only one file can be uploaded. Review of applications will begin on October 23, 2026 and continue until the position has been filled. St. Francis Xavier University is located in Mi’kma’ki, the ancestral and unceded territory of the Mi’kmaq People. Our institution is committed to upholding the values of equity, diversity, inclusion and accessibility. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been historically disadvantaged and marginalized, including Indigenous persons (especially Mi’kmaq), racialized persons (especially African Nova Scotians), persons with disabilities, those who identify as women and/or 2SLGBTQIA+ and any others who would contribute to the diversity of our community. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. We are also committed to the elimination of barriers to participation for persons with disabilities. Should you require an accommodation during the recruitment process, please contact People and Culture at hr@stfx. Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications St. Francis Xavier University You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.ca/GOV5000GOVE/JobBoard/b0aac1dd-538d-4eb2-91aa-e8ce062974c9/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=cd2afb8d-237a-4dd5-8f30-ea2b72ea4add": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher | Career Opportunities Skip to main content Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Postdoctoral Researcher Job Category : Research Requisition Number : POSTD001765 Apply now Posting Details Posted : October 9, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Postdoctoral Researcher Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences Duration: 24-month term beginning, January 1, 2027 (negotiable) Classification: Contractual term position (37.5 hours/week) Salary: $65,000 per year plus benefits Overview As Canada moves to implement the use of lower-carbon energy sources, there will be a significant increase in the demand for critical elements associated with energy production and storage. Canada lists 31 minerals and chemical elements that are essential to the country’s future. Many critical elements are concentrated by igneous processes (e.g., Li, Sn, W, Mo). The Canadian Appalachians as well as correlative rocks in the Caledonides of the British Isles are known to host to igneous rocks that are with critical mineral endowments. The postdoctoral researcher (PDR) will contribute to a research program on the igneous and tectonic processes that concentrate critical minerals. The project area is Nova Scotia, Canada. The main program objective is to better understand the geological controls on formation of granite-related critical mineral systems rocks to better inform exploration activities. The PDR will have the opportunity to collaborate with industry partners invested mineral exploration in Nova Scotia. Key Responsibilities Specific responsibilities of the PDR are to: Synthesize published literature with new data to investigate selected critical mineral occurrences in Nova Scotia. Assist in establishing a new scanning-electron-microscope facility at StFX University and developing analytical protocols to advance the application of SEM for studying geological processes. The PDR will also have opportunities to mentor undergraduate and graduate student researchers. Qualifications Completion of a doctoral degree in Geology, Earth Sciences, or a related field at the time of appointment is required. Expertise in one or more of the following broad fields is considered an asset: Igneous petrology, geochronology, mineralogy, or tectonics. Experience using a scanning electron microscope is also considered an asset. Application Instructions Applications will be considered immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply please submit a letter of application, CV including publications list, and the names and contact information of two referees to: Dr. Donnelly Archibald darchiba@stfx.ca The cover letter should demonstrate how the candidate meets the above-mentioned criteria. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. Canadian applicants and permanent residents will be given priority. StFX is in Mi’kma’ki, the ancestral and unceded territory of the Mi’kmaq People. StFX is seeking candidates who are committed to contributing to our priorities of equity, diversity, and inclusion. A demonstrated ability in fostering a climate of inclusion and cultural safety is a strong asset. Our institution is committed to upholding the values of equity, diversity, inclusion and accessibility. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been historically disadvantaged and marginalized, including Indigenous persons (especially Mi’kmaq), racialized persons (especially African Nova Scotians), persons with disabilities, those who identify as women and/or 2SLGBTQIA+ and any others who would contribute to the diversity of our community. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. We are also committed to the elimination of barriers to participation for persons with disabilities. Should you require an accommodation during the recruitment process, please contact People and Culture at hr@stfx.ca or 902-867-5038. About St. Francis Xavier University Established in 1853, St. Francis Xavier University (StFX) is consistently recognized as one of the best universities in Canada. StFX exceeds the needs of today’s undergraduates through providing the very best academic experience -- outstanding teaching, exceptional hands-on research opportunities, and global exchanges -- all within Canada’s most vibrant and inspiring residential campus. Here, the focus is on the academic and the personal development of every student, making community and social engagement a large part of the learning experience. Our students are academically strong and highly engaged in every aspect of life, determined to make a positive impact on the world. In 2022, Maclean's annual university rankings put StFX #1 in student satisfaction and #2 in reputation across Canada in the primarily undergraduate category. For more, visit www.stfx.ca. As a member of one of the most research-intensive departments on the StFX campus, you will have the opportunity to work on an innovative research project. StFX offers graduate studies at the MSc and PhD levels, and many graduate students are active within the department. For more information, visit https://www.stfx.ca/department/earth-environmental-sciences/research-graduate-studies. About Antigonish, Nova Scotia The beautiful and historic town of Antigonish is located within the northeastern corner of Nova Scotia. Only a 15-minute drive from the ocean and a two-hour drive to major urban centres, Antigonish is a family-friendly community with a diverse and growing population and provides access to excellent education at all levels. The town is surrounded by rolling countryside and beautiful beaches and is a hotspot for outdoor recreation, sports, and other active lifestyle activities. Antigonish supports a vibrant culture in the arts with music, performing arts, visual arts and crafts, and fabulous festivals happening throughout the year. For more information visit www.townofantigonish.ca or www.visitantigonish.ca . Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications St. Francis Xavier University You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.ca/GOV5000GOVE/JobBoard/b0aac1dd-538d-4eb2-91aa-e8ce062974c9/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=96ec8dff-3d05-436f-936d-d364fbcece45": {
      "title": "Tier 1 Canada Research Chair (CRC) Canadian Institute of Health Research",
      "description": "Tier 1 Canada Research Chair (CRC)   Canadian Institute of Health Research – CIHR | Career Opportunities Skip to main content Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Tier 1 Canada Research Chair (CRC)   Canadian Institute of Health Research – CIHR Job Category : Faculty and Academic Staff Requisition Number : TIER1001757 Apply now Posting Details Posted : October 9, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description St. Francis Xavier University (StFX) invites applications from candidates (external or internal) interested in being nominated for a Tier 1 Canada Research Chair (CRC) in any Canadian Institute of Health Research (CIHR) discipline. Nominees must hold the rank of professor or be an associate professor who is eligible to be promoted to the rank of professor within one to two years of the nomination. Tier 1 CRCs, tenable for 7 years and renewable once, are for established world leaders in their fields with superior records of research, training, and innovation . Specifically, to meet the criteria of the program, Tier 1 CRC nominees must: be outstanding and innovative world-class researchers whose accomplishments have made a major impact in their fields; be recognized internationally as leaders in their fields; have superior records of attracting and supervising graduate students and postdoctoral fellows (taking into account different practices in the relevant field or discipline) and, as chairholders, be expected to attract, develop and retain excellent trainees, students and future researchers; and be proposing an original, innovative research program of the highest quality. Potential applicants should consult the Canada Research Chairs website for full program information, including further details on eligibility criteria. Applicants may wish to consult the detailed instructions for what will be expected as full submission requirements to the CRC program for each of the Quality of Nominee and Research Program Description , and the CV details . Tier 1 CRC positions are intended for established researchers acknowledged by their peers as world leaders in their field. If coming from outside the academic sector, candidates must possess equivalent qualifications required for appointment at the full or associate professor level. We strongly encourage all Tier 1 nominees to advance their applications in line with the research and innovation priorities outlines in StFX’s Teliaq Ta’n Teluek Strategic Research & Innovation Plan 2026–2031 . Please contact David Bruce, Director of Research Grants (at dwbruce@stfx.ca ) for more information. StFX is committed to the principles of equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility (EDIA). Women and gender minorities, persons with disabilities, Indigenous Peoples, and racialized people are encouraged to apply.  The selection process will include consideration of alignment with the goals of the StFX CRC Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) Action Plan , and the EDIA requirements of the CRC program. The successful candidate will be nominated for a 7-year Tier 1 CRC term starting September 1, 2027. The nomination by the university will be submitted to the Canada Research Chairs program by March 1, 2027. Application Process: Expression of interest applications must include, as a single PDF file: A statement outlining the Quality of Nominee (maximum 1 page). This statement should demonstrate that you are an excellent world-class researcher who has demonstrated outstanding international research activity in your fields as acknowledged by your peers. A description of the Proposed Research Program for the initial 7-year CRC term (minimum 3 pages, maximum 6 pages). This section should include details on Context, Methods, Communication of Results Plans, and HQP Training Plans and should be written to describe the proposed program of research in a way that allows informed assessment by committee members who may not have direct expertise in the area. A CV (any length, in any format is acceptable), including applicable leaves that have resulted in career interruptions. If applicable, a statement of possible requests for new research equipment required to fulfill the proposed CRC program of research, which may form the basis of a simultaneous CRC-Canada Foundation for Innovation (CFI) application. As a requirement for this opportunity, shortlisted candidates will be required to give an oral presentation to the university community demonstrating how they meet the criteria for the CIHR Tier 1 CRC program as outlined above. Applications will be reviewed and ranked by a committee of CIHR subject matter experts approved by the StFX Research Advisory Committee (RAC), which will make a recommendation to the Academic Vice President and Provost. In its evaluation process, the review committee will follow the principles outlined in the CRC Guide for Creating an Equitable, Diverse and Inclusive Research Environment: A Best Practices Guide for Recruitment, Hiring and Retention. As part of their application package, candidates applying externally to StFX are asked to please identify up to two academic departments where their research most clearly aligns: https://www.stfx.ca/programs-courses/programs In this evaluation, the committee will be mindful that the best-qualified candidates may not have the most years of experience, greatest number of publications, or largest number of academic accomplishments. For example, an applicant who took time away from work or studies for family-related matters or a person with disabilities who has a reduced workload may not have as many publications, but the substance and quality of that applicant’s work may render them best qualified. The committee will also evaluate candidates at different career stages by recognizing differences in research outputs and accomplishments, as well as recognize the value of research outputs that are in different formats or platforms. The review committee will recognize and take into consideration differences between research norms in different disciplines: different forms of research output and different modes of evaluating research. Application packages must be submitted no later than November 12, 2026 (5:00 PM AST) via email to: Dr. Erin Morton Associate Vice President Research, Graduate and Professional Studies Please submit applications to the following email address: emorton@stfx.ca with copy to Ms. Navneet Kaur nkaur@stfx.ca and Mr. David Bruce dwbruce@stfx.ca Interested applicants are encouraged to meet with the Director of Research Grants, Mr. David Bruce, to discuss a potential application, and to obtain answers to any questions they may have about the CRC program. Please contact David Bruce at dwbruce@stfx.ca to arrange a meeting. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply. StFX is committed to the elimination of barriers to participation for persons with disabilities. Should you require an accommodation in preparing this application or during the internal selection process, please contact People and Culture at hr@stfx.ca or 902-867-5038. In accordance with the StFX CRC Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Action Plan , applicants to CRC positions will be contacted by the Office of Human Rights and Equity’s personnel and asked to complete a voluntary self-identification questionnaire. These data are shared in aggregate only (i.e., identifying information will never be shared) to the selection committee and Academic Vice-President and Provost’s office to monitor the extent of diversity in the pool of applicants. The successful applicant will be required to provide additional self-identification information to the CRC program as part of the nomination process. This opportunity was posted on the StFX website on October 9, 2026.  Any questions regarding the process to select the nominee for this CRC position can be directed to Dr. Erin Morton, Associate Vice President, Research, Graduate and Professional Studies, at emorton@stfx.ca . Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications St. Francis Xavier University You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.ca/GOV5000GOVE/JobBoard/b0aac1dd-538d-4eb2-91aa-e8ce062974c9/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=52f1090c-6e22-4ffd-8829-101a3541c2fb": {
      "title": "Part-time Academic Instructor",
      "description": "Part-time Academic Instructor - Math 236 | Career Opportunities Skip to main content Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Part-time Academic Instructor - Math 236 Job Category : Faculty and Academic Staff Requisition Number : ASSIS001784 Apply now Posting Details Posted : October 22, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN Nova Scotia St. Francis Xavier University 4130 University Avenue Antigonish, NS B2G2W5, CAN +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Faculty of Science Part-time Academic Instructor - Math 236 (Data Modeling for Business) Position Description The Department of Mathematics & Statistics at St. Francis Xavier University invites applications for a part-time academic instructor to teach Math 236 (Data Modeling for Business) (3 credits) in the winter term of the 2026-2027 academic year.  This position is subject to final budgetary approval. We seek applicants with a foundational background in mathematics or statistics, normally an undergraduate degree in mathematics, statistics, or related field, and a graduate degree, normally a minimum of a Master’s degree, and/or a professional degree or designation relevant to the instruction of this course. Preference will be given to individuals who have expertise in mathematics or statistics, who have previous classroom teaching experience and strong communication skills. The successful candidate should employ an equitable, diverse, inclusive, and accessible teaching approach to promote a respectful environment for all students, staff, and faculty. Course Description: Evidence-based decision-making in business required the use of the mathematical models to analyze data and to help identify and assess possible answers to what-if questions. This course introduces the student to what should be considered when using mathematical models for business. Topics include model construction, analyzing and modeling data sets, optimization, risk analysis and model testing. Prerequisite: One of MATH 105, 106 or 126. Start and End Dates: January 1, 2027 to April 30, 2027 Class Schedule: W4/W5 blocks (Mondays 1:00-2:15pm, Wednesdays 11:30am-12:45pm) Delivery Mode: In-person About the Department To learn more about the Department of Mathematics and Statistics at St Francis Xavier University, please visit our website: https://www.stfx.ca/department/mathematics-statistics Application Instructions The review of applications will commence November 10, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit a cover letter (outlining one’s suitability for the position), a curriculum vitae, a statement on teaching experiences, interests, and teaching philosophy, as well as the names and email addresses of three references via email to: Dr. Tara Taylor Department Chair, Mathematics & Statistics St. Francis Xavier University 2323 Notre Dame Avenue, Antigonish, Nova Scotia, B2G 2W5 math@stfx.ca St. Francis Xavier University is located in Mi’kma’ki, the ancestral and unceded territory of the Mi’kmaq People. Our institution is committed to upholding the values of equity, diversity, inclusion and accessibility. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been historically disadvantaged and marginalized, including Indigenous persons (especially Mi’kmaq), racialized persons (especially African Nova Scotians), persons with disabilities, those who identify as women and/or 2SLGBTQIA+ and any others who would contribute to the diversity of our community. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. We are also committed to the elimination of barriers to participation for persons with disabilities. Should you require an accommodation during the recruitment process, please contact People and Culture at hr@stfx.ca or 902-867-5038. About St. Francis Xavier University Established in 1853, St. Francis Xavier University (StFX) is consistently recognized as one of the best universities in Canada. StFX exceeds the needs of today’s undergraduates through providing the very best academic experience -- outstanding teaching, exceptional hands-on research opportunities, and global exchanges -- all within Canada’s most vibrant and inspiring residential campus. Here, the focus is on the academic and the personal development of every student, making community and social engagement a large part of the learning experience. Our students are academically strong and highly engaged in every aspect of life, determined to make a positive impact on the world. In 2022, Maclean's annual university rankings put StFX #1 in student satisfaction and #2 in reputation across Canada in the primarily undergraduate category. For more, visit www.stfx.ca . About Antigonish, Nova Scotia The beautiful and historic town of Antigonish is located within the northeastern corner of Nova Scotia. Only a 15-minute drive from the ocean and a two-hour drive to major urban centres, Antigonish is a family-friendly community with a diverse and growing population and provides access to excellent education at all levels. The town is surrounded by rolling countryside and beautiful beaches and is a hotspot for outdoor recreation, sports and other active lifestyle activities. Antigonish supports a vibrant culture in the arts with music, performing arts, visual arts and crafts, and fabulous festivals happening throughout the year. For more information visit www.townofantigonish.ca or www.visitantigonish.ca . Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications St. Francis Xavier University You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
